@page "/comms"
@using DYS.Molargo.Shared.Features.Comms.Components
@using DYS.Molargo.Shared.Features.Comms.ViewModels
@inherits MvvmComponentBase<CommsViewModel>

@*
    The prototype's communication and marketing screen: heading with the six tabs, then
    the pane.

    Templates, campaign audiences, the portal inbox and consent are real. Nothing sends —
    there is no SMS or email gateway and no scheduler, both of which are online work — so
    each pane says where its usefulness stops rather than implying a send happened.

    The one thing this screen must never do is imply a message went out. A practice that
    believes its reminders are being sent stops phoning to confirm, and the cost lands as
    empty chairs.
*@

<PageTitle>Comms — Molargo</PageTitle>

<div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-5 border-b-2 border-divider px-6 py-3">
    <h4 class="m-0">Comms</h4>

    <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-4">
        @foreach (var tab in Tabs)
        {
            <button class="@TabCss(tab.Value)"
                    @onclick="() => ViewModel.SelectTabCommand.ExecuteAsync(tab.Value)">
                @tab.Label
            </button>
        }
    </div>

    @*
        The design puts a live gateway status here. Molargo has no gateway, so the header
        carries the fact instead — in the one place that is visible from every pane.
    *@
    <span class="ml-auto text-xs font-semibold text-accent-800">
        No gateway connected — nothing is sent from this screen
    </span>
</div>

@*
    A failure wins over the last success rather than stacking under it: a refused save
    does not clear the previous action's confirmation, and the two together read as
    though both had happened.
*@
@if (ViewModel.HasError)
{
    <NoticeComponent Kind="NoticeKind.Failure"
                     Headline="@ViewModel.ErrorHeadline"
                     Message="@ViewModel.ErrorMessage" />
}
else if (ViewModel.LastAction is { Length: > 0 } action)
{
    <NoticeComponent Message="@action" />
}

@switch (ViewModel.Tab)
{
    case CommsTab.Templates:
        <TemplateEditorComponent Vm="ViewModel" />
        break;

    case CommsTab.Campaigns:
        <CampaignComponent Vm="ViewModel" />
        break;

    case CommsTab.Automated:
        <AutomatedMessagesComponent Vm="ViewModel" />
        break;

    case CommsTab.Inbox:
        <PortalInboxComponent Vm="ViewModel" />
        break;

    case CommsTab.Preferences:
        <ConsentComponent Vm="ViewModel" />
        break;

    default:
        @*
            Reviews. The design shows a request funnel — 186 requested, 92 clicked, 44
            reviewed — plus Google and Facebook star ratings and a Mailchimp connection.

            Every number there comes from outside: the click-through needs a tracked link
            the practice owns, the ratings need the Google Business and Facebook APIs, and
            the newsletter needs a Mailchimp account. Inventing the funnel would put a
            conversion rate on screen that no send produced, which is the kind of figure a
            practice makes a marketing decision on.
        *@
        <div class="max-w-[720px] px-6 py-6">
            <h5>Reviews &amp; newsletter</h5>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink/55">
                A review funnel needs three things Molargo does not have: a way to send the
                request, a tracked link to know it was clicked, and the Google Business and
                Facebook APIs to read back the rating that resulted. The design's
                under-4★ routing — private feedback form and a manager task instead of the
                public link — needs the same send path.
            </p>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink/55">
                The newsletter panel is a Mailchimp integration: an audience synced both
                ways so an unsubscribe there becomes an opt-out here. Consent is recorded
                in <strong>Preferences</strong> and would be the local half of that sync;
                nothing syncs today.
            </p>
            <button class="btn btn-secondary"
                    @onclick="() => ViewModel.SelectTabCommand.ExecuteAsync(CommsTab.Preferences)">
                Communication preferences →
            </button>
        </div>
        break;
}

<div class="px-6 pb-6 font-mono text-[11px] text-neutral-700">
    WEB · COMMS
</div>

@code {
    private sealed record TabDefinition(string Label, CommsTab Value);

    /// <summary>The prototype's six tabs, in its order.</summary>
    private static readonly TabDefinition[] Tabs =
    [
        new("Templates", CommsTab.Templates),
        new("Campaigns", CommsTab.Campaigns),
        new("Automated", CommsTab.Automated),
        new("Reviews", CommsTab.Reviews),
        new("Inbox", CommsTab.Inbox),
        new("Preferences", CommsTab.Preferences),
    ];

    private string TabCss(CommsTab tab) =>
        ViewModel.Tab == tab
            ? "-mb-0.5 cursor-pointer border-0 border-b-[3px] border-accent bg-transparent px-0 py-3 font-[inherit] text-[13px] font-bold text-ink"
            : "-mb-0.5 cursor-pointer border-0 border-b-[3px] border-transparent bg-transparent px-0 py-3 font-[inherit] text-[13px] text-ink hover:text-accent";
}
